      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How do machines store numbers",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How do machines store numbers",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "pLIOq0_nAYkX_yEBRsOai",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 295.9771216710409,
      "y": 475.5409711201987,
      "width": 731.7780761718747,
      "height": 1000,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a4",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 335268488,
      "version": 1206,
      "versionNonce": 1288924683,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788613633048,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The most fundamental unit of representation for computers is the bit.\nHowever the representation varies whether the numbers in question are\nintegers or real numbers.\n\nInteger representations: \n    - INT8\n    - INT16 \n    - INT32 \n    - etc\nThey are simply represented as a sequence of bits.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nA common way to  representing real numbers is through floating-point: the\nnumber of digits for representing the integer and the fractional parts can\nvary. \n    - FP16 (half-precision)\n    - FP32 (full-precision)\n    - bFP16\n\nThere are three components in the floating-point representation:\n    1. The sign bit: this is the bit specifying the sign of the number.\n    2. The exponent part\n    3. The mantissa/fraction/significant\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The most fundamental unit of representation for computers is the bit. However the representation varies whether the numbers in question are integers or real numbers.\n\nInteger representations: \n    - INT8\n    - INT16 \n    - INT32 \n    - etc\nThey are simply represented as a sequence of bits.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nA common way to  representing real numbers is through floating-point: the number of digits for representing the integer and the fractional parts can vary. \n    - FP16 (half-precision)\n    - FP32 (full-precision)\n    - bFP16\n\nThere are three components in the floating-point representation:\n    1. The sign bit: this is the bit specifying the sign of the number.\n    2. The exponent part\n    3. The mantissa/fraction/significant\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "LQ842x3hz8lB54lVNh0K8",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 265.7195424806504,
      "y": 2300.5216286295936,
      "width": 329.1973876953125,
