      "updated": 1788646598474,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Calibration",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Calibration",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "5y69qR1LMObwMsKAVeCh2",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 101.93540811538674,
      "y": 4518.0102314949045,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 825,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aE",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 184998136,
      "version": 2483,
      "versionNonce": 1674890931,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788646598474,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Calibration is the process of finding alpha and beta. \n\nA simple approach would be taking the minimum ancd maximum values in the model weights.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe problem is that very sensitive to outliers: there might be gaps between the alpha and the central\nvalues, which leads to wasting a lot of space in the quintized  space. \n\nWe have to make most of the use of tehe few bits we have!\n\nTo minimze this waste we could ignore the outliers.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe trade off is that we are loosing accuracy because all the outliers to the left and right will be\nclamped to q_min and q_max.\n\nThe only way to minimize this is traying different therholds and measure the difference between the\ninput and output distrubutino. To do this we need a measure of distribuiiotn, Kullback-Leibler (KL)\ndivergence, and a calibration dataset.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Calibration is the process of finding alpha and beta. \n\nA simple approach would be taking the minimum ancd maximum values in the model weights.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe problem is that very sensitive to outliers: there might be gaps between the alpha and the central values, which leads to wasting a lot of space in the quintized  space. \n\nWe have to make most of the use of tehe few bits we have!\n\nTo minimze this waste we could ignore the outliers.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe trade off is that we are loosing accuracy because all the outliers to the left and right will be clamped to q_min and q_max.\n\nThe only way to minimize this is traying different therholds and measure the difference between the input and output distrubutino. To do this we need a measure of distribuiiotn, Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence, and a calibration dataset.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
