    },
    {
      "id": "aBf_t5lXk64OF4Sgqgp43",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2137.3934119939795,
      "y": 936.5135203997288,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 650,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "ab",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1520303051,
      "version": 2303,
      "versionNonce": 1730754987,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788519014524,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "training\n- Historically  in full precision\n- present in half precision with bfloat16\n- recent llm (deepseekR1 and llama4) rained on float8 but no public procedure\n\nInference is more resilient in loss in precision.\n\n\nPost-training dynamic quantization\n- load the trained model and convert modle weights and activations ahead of time\n- rescale activations on the fly, just before running the computation\n- the scale factor is computed dynamically, which is simple, more flexible but has some overhead\n\nPost-training statis quantization\n- load the trained model and convert modle weights and activations ahead of time\n- predict a calibration dataset, and use the observed distribuitino of activations to set the scale\nfactor\n- no overhead and lower latency, calibration may not work well fot all datasets\n\nQuantization aware training\nSometimes trainng is done taking into account future quantization\n- train still at full precision\n- in the training apply quantization to weight and activations (???)\n- higher quality models, but need to retrain which is expensive for LLM\n\nAt inference time, a dequantization process retuns results in the original data type",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "training\n- Historically  in full precision\n- present in half precision with bfloat16\n- recent llm (deepseekR1 and llama4) rained on float8 but no public procedure\n\nInference is more resilient in loss in precision.\n\n\nPost-training dynamic quantization\n- load the trained model and convert modle weights and activations ahead of time\n- rescale activations on the fly, just before running the computation\n- the scale factor is computed dynamically, which is simple, more flexible but has some overhead\n\nPost-training statis quantization\n- load the trained model and convert modle weights and activations ahead of time\n- predict a calibration dataset, and use the observed distribuitino of activations to set the scale factor\n- no overhead and lower latency, calibration may not work well fot all datasets\n\nQuantization aware training\nSometimes trainng is done taking into account future quantization\n- train still at full precision\n- in the training apply quantization to weight and activations (???)\n- higher quality models, but need to retrain which is expensive for LLM\n\nAt inference time, a dequantization process retuns results in the original data type",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
