    {
      "id": "ELmaoDZTW1f1y4c_MZmRO",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2176.733317880401,
      "y": 1773.9871666999097,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 375,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0R",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 854955531,
      "version": 1138,
      "versionNonce": 1334171405,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788802663759,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- Dynamic post-training quantization\n- quantization primitives for INT8/INT4 linear layers (weights onòly), and INT8 optimizers for training\n\nThe Importance of outlires:\n1. Above 6B outliers are systematic, not random.\nIn large Transformers, extreme activations repeatedly appear in the same hidden feature dimensions,\nacross many layers and many sequence positions. They are therefore a structural property of the\nrepresentation, not isolated numerical accidents.\n\n\n\n- vector-wise quantization: use diffreent scaling factors when multiplying input and weight tensors\n- outliers dimansions are quantized separately with mixed-precision decomposiiton (16bit)\n- 8bit quantization vs FP16: 2x memory saving, accuracy on par\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- Dynamic post-training quantization\n- quantization primitives for INT8/INT4 linear layers (weights onòly), and INT8 optimizers for training\n\nThe Importance of outlires:\n1. Above 6B outliers are systematic, not random.\nIn large Transformers, extreme activations repeatedly appear in the same hidden feature dimensions, across many layers and many sequence positions. They are therefore a structural property of the representation, not isolated numerical accidents.\n\n\n\n- vector-wise quantization: use diffreent scaling factors when multiplying input and weight tensors\n- outliers dimansions are quantized separately with mixed-precision decomposiiton (16bit)\n- 8bit quantization vs FP16: 2x memory saving, accuracy on par\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
