    {
      "id": "xa6Bk9JQeLsbL-ZhJ8JlW",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 4428.06132534004,
      "y": 806.088234799252,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 925,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0g",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2117462731,
      "version": 3212,
      "versionNonce": 1965470603,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788603910484,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- Static post-training quantization\n- wrote a alternative to pytorchin C and C++ with all th ebasic algebra to run depp learning models on\nCPU\n- GGUF is a binary format that stores the quantized weights\n- show the workflow, link to ollama\n\n_ Different quantization algorithms\n    1. legacy\n    2. K quants\n    3. Q quants\n\nLagacy quants\n- type 0, symmetric\n- type 1, asymmetric\n- use block quantization, block size is a nob, bigger means less quantization constants (save space) but\nloss granularity. For a block size 32 and a model of 16B need 2B just for the quantizatoin constants\non top of the weights. K solves this.\n\nK Quants\nthe solution is quantize the quantization constants\nNow the overhead is only 1GB\nThis tw level quantization is also known in the Qlora paper as Double Quantizaton\n\nI- Quants\n???\nIt uses the importance matrix of AWQ\nIn a first step the importance matrix is calculated with a calibration dataset\n\nRecal the improvemebts\n- linear quantization\n- super block quantization\n- vector quant\n\n+ importance matrix as general improvement\n\nFinally the modifiers. There is amixed precision in the model (show them in an image), the size indicates\nloosly how much precision",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- Static post-training quantization\n- wrote a alternative to pytorchin C and C++ with all th ebasic algebra to run depp learning models on CPU\n- GGUF is a binary format that stores the quantized weights\n- show the workflow, link to ollama\n\n_ Different quantization algorithms\n    1. legacy\n    2. K quants\n    3. Q quants\n\nLagacy quants\n- type 0, symmetric\n- type 1, asymmetric\n- use block quantization, block size is a nob, bigger means less quantization constants (save space) but loss granularity. For a block size 32 and a model of 16B need 2B just for the quantizatoin constants on top of the weights. K solves this.\n\nK Quants\nthe solution is quantize the quantization constants\nNow the overhead is only 1GB\nThis tw level quantization is also known in the Qlora paper as Double Quantizaton\n\nI- Quants\n???\nIt uses the importance matrix of AWQ\nIn a first step the importance matrix is calculated with a calibration dataset\n\nRecal the improvemebts\n- linear quantization\n- super block quantization\n- vector quant\n\n+ importance matrix as general improvement\n\nFinally the modifiers. There is amixed precision in the model (show them in an image), the size indicates loosly how much precision",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
